diff --git a/doc/Manual/ManualCreditControl.html b/doc/Manual/ManualCreditControl.html
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..620c4e7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/Manual/ManualCreditControl.html
@@ -0,0 +1,81 @@
+<h1><a id="CreditControl">Credit Control</a></h1>
+
+<h2>Overview</h2>
+<p>Credit control applies the same rules to every customer every time, and keeps a record of every decision: who made it, when and why.
+It covers credit reviews, stopping and watching customers, overdue reminder letters, month end interest and late fees, bad debt write-offs
+and the credit control report. Everything is found in the Accounts Receivable menu (Transactions, Reports and Maintenance) and nothing
+needs to be done outside webERP except the collection of debts by agencies or lawyers, which can be noted against the customer.</p>
+
+<p>Who can do what is controlled by two security tokens, given to user roles in <i>User Roles Maintenance</i>:</p>
+<ul>
+	<li><b>Credit Control</b> - the credit controller, the deputy and the Finance Director. Only these users can change credit limits and credit status, run reminders and month end charges, and request write-offs. Sales reps and the sales office cannot.</li>
+	<li><b>Credit Control Write-off Approval</b> - the Finance Director. Write-offs above the amount set by Finance wait for this approval. Nobody can approve a write-off they asked for themselves.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2><a id="CreditSettings">Credit Control Settings</a></h2>
+<p>Finance sets, in <i>Accounts Receivable &gt; Maintenance &gt; Credit Control Settings</i>: the standard review period in months (new customers get their first
+review date from it), the interest rate per year, the late fee per month (in the home currency, converted to the customer's currency), the number of days overdue
+before a balance is charged, the general ledger accounts that interest and fees are posted to, the bad debt account, the amount above which write-offs need approval,
+the minimum number of days between two reminders to the same customer, and whether a customer is put on stop automatically after the final notice.</p>
+<p>The wording and timing of the reminder letters is set in <i>Reminder Letter Stages</i>: any number of stages, the days overdue that trigger each, and the text in each language
+(customers whose language has no text get the English one). In the text <tt>{name} {invoices} {currency} {amount} {company} {date}</tt> are replaced when the letter is made.</p>
+
+<h2><a id="CreditReview">Credit reviews</a></h2>
+<p>Every customer on account has a next review date. Use <i>Credit Review</i> to record a review: the new credit limit, the credit status, the notes and the next review date.
+The credit limit changes at the same time as the review is recorded, so the limit and the reason for it are always kept together. The outcome (limit confirmed, raised,
+reduced, put on watch, put on stop, taken off stop) follows from what was changed. Reviews can never be changed or deleted, the history of reviews is shown on the same page.
+In the same page invoices can be marked as <i>disputed</i> - they are then not chased by reminders and not charged interest until the dispute is settled -
+and customers can be exempted from interest and/or fees. A note can be added at any time, for example to record that a debt has been passed to a collection agency.</p>
+
+<h2><a id="StopWatch">Stop and watch</a></h2>
+<p>A customer's credit status is one of the credit statuses set up in <i>Credit Status Code Maintenance</i>. A status marked <i>Disallow Invoices</i> is <b>on stop</b>, any other
+status is normal; statuses defined with the value 2 for disallow invoices (customers to be watched) show a warning. To put a customer on stop or on watch, or to take them off,
+record a credit review with the status and the reason.</p>
+<ul>
+	<li><b>On stop</b>: no orders can be accepted by any route - the sales office, the customer in the web shop, electronic orders and standing orders - and nothing is dispatched or
+	invoiced on orders taken earlier unless credit control releases the order in <i>Orders Held or On Watch for Credit Control</i>. A customer in the web shop is told that the account is on hold.</li>
+	<li><b>On watch</b>: the customer is still supplied, but whoever takes an order sees a warning, and credit control sees the orders in <i>Orders Held or On Watch for Credit Control</i>.
+	Customers are not shown that they are on watch.</li>
+	<li>The credit status is shown on the customer inquiry and account screens, and in the aged debtors report together with the credit limit and the last reminder stage sent.</li>
+	<li>Orders that take a customer over the credit limit are held for credit control approval when <i>Check Credit Limits</i> in the system parameters is set to <i>Hold order for credit control approval</i>.
+	They are released in <i>Orders Held or On Watch for Credit Control</i>.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2><a id="ReminderRun">The reminder run</a></h2>
+<p>Credit control starts <i>Overdue Reminder Run</i> regularly, for example every week. It first shows which customers would get which letter, to whom it would be emailed and
+the overdue amount. Untick the customers who must not be chased in this run - for example where a payment has been promised - and send. Letters are emailed to the customer's statement contacts
+(the contacts ticked for statements). Customers with no email address are listed with the text of the letter so that it can be posted.</p>
+<p>A customer only moves up a stage if they still have overdue invoices, so a customer who has paid gets nothing, and the same stage is not sent twice, nor any letter within the
+minimum number of days after the last one. Disputed invoices are not chased. Every letter sent is recorded against the customer with the date, the stage, the invoices and the amount, and
+customers can see their letters in the customer portal.</p>
+
+<h2><a id="MonthEndCharges">Month end interest and late fees</a></h2>
+<p>At month end open <i>Month End Interest and Late Fees</i>, choose the month and look at the list of what will be charged to whom. Untick any customer you do not want to charge and confirm.
+Interest is simple interest at the yearly rate on the balance that is overdue by at least the number of days set by Finance, for the number of days in the month; unallocated receipts and credits
+reduce that balance. Interest is not charged on late fees or interest. The late fee is charged once per customer per month. Exempt customers and disputed invoices are left out.
+Charges are entered in the customer's own currency as separate items (<i>Late Payment Interest</i> and <i>Late Payment Fee</i>) that show on the customer account, the next statement and in the portal,
+and are posted to the general ledger. A month is never charged twice to the same customer. A charge can be waived with a reason from the same screen; the waiver reverses the charge in the customer account and in the ledger.</p>
+
+<h2><a id="WriteOffs">Bad debt write-offs</a></h2>
+<p>Use <i>Bad Debt Write-offs</i> (or the link in Credit Review) to write off all or part of a customer's balance with a reason. A write-off below the approval amount is posted at once; a larger one
+waits in the list for the Finance Director to approve or reject it, and nothing is posted until then. A posted write-off reduces the customer's open invoices, oldest first, and is posted to debtors control and the bad debt account;
+any difference between the exchange rate of the invoices and the rate of the write-off goes to the exchange difference account. The customer is put on stop unless the option is cleared. The same page reports the
+write-offs in a period: customer, amount, reason, who asked and who approved. When a debt that was written off is paid later, enter the receipt as usual: it appears as an unallocated receipt on the account.</p>
+
+<h2><a id="CreditControlReport">Credit control report</a></h2>
+<p>The report lists customers due or overdue for a credit review, customers over their credit limit and by how much, and customers on watch or on stop. It can be filtered by sales rep and currency, and the totals
+are in the home currency. Sales reps only see their own customers. <i>Credit Control History</i> shows everything that has happened to one customer: reviews, status and limit changes, reminders, charges, waivers and write-offs.</p>
+
+<h2><a id="MonthEndReconciliation">Month end reconciliation</a></h2>
+<p>At month end the total of the customer balances must agree with the debtors control account. <i>Debtors Control Integrity</i> (special utilities) now includes interest, fees, waivers and write-offs,
+and where the two disagree it lists the customer account entries that have no posting to the control account and the postings to the control account that are not in any customer account.</p>
+
+<h2><a id="GoLive">Going live</a></h2>
+<p>When the update is applied every existing customer keeps their balances and history, starts as normal with a next review date 12 months ahead, and over-limit orders begin to be held.
+Use <i>Import Customer Credit Status from .csv file</i> to load the stop list, the credit limits and the review dates from the credit controller's spreadsheet: customer code, status (normal, watch or stop),
+credit limit, next review date as YYYY-MM-DD and a note. The file is checked first and only loaded when the confirm box is ticked.</p>
+
+<h2><a id="SalesNote">A note for sales staff</a></h2>
+<p><b>On watch</b> means we still supply the customer, but you will see a warning when you take an order for them. Talk to the customer, and tell credit control about the order if you are worried.
+<b>On stop</b> means no orders can be taken for the customer by anybody. You can see the reason; you cannot change it. Do not promise delivery - contact credit control, who decide whether to release orders.
+Over-limit orders are accepted but held until credit control approves them. You can see the credit information of your own customers only.</p>
